  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Friday) \nIssued at 1244 PM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nAt least one more record day is in store on Saturday as surface low \npressure advances eastward off the High Plains, further tightening \nthe pressure gradient leading to gusty southwesterly winds across \nmuch of eastern Oklahoma. The gusty winds and continued lower \ndewpoints will lead to heightened fire weather concerns Saturday \nafternoon for both grassland and timber fuels, especially for the \nmore drought stricken areas. Remain aware of ongoing burn bans \nacross the area.\n\nA welcome pattern change will arrive beginning late Saturday night in \nthe form of a cold front. Showers and thunderstorms are forecast to \ndevelop west of the forecast area Saturday evening and progress \neastward into Saturday night. These initial storms will likely stall \nout and dissipate mainly north of I-44 Saturday night into early \nSunday morning. Another uptick in showers and storms will then \naccompany the actual cold front later Sunday morning into Sunday \nafternoon. Storm coverage will then spread southeastward with the \nslowly advancing frontal boundary through early Monday. A few strong \nto severe storms will be possible during the day Sunday, along with \nlocally heavy rainfall due to the slow frontal progression. \n\nRain chances will linger in to Monday as cooler, more seasonable air \nfilters into the region. Cloudiness and cooler surface high pressure \nsettling over the area could keep temperatures in the 70s Monday and \nTuesday, especially north of I-40, marking a notable cooldown \ncompared to previous weeks. The more seasonal conditions look to \npersist through much of next week.\n\nBowlan",
